mkc - exact DNA k-mer counting in C
===================================

mkc counts every canonical k-mer (1 <= k <= 32) of FASTA/FASTQ input, plain
or compressed (gzip, BGZF, zstd, lz4), and writes a memory-mapped index
answering point queries.

Build requirements: a C99 compiler with the GCC/Clang extensions (GCC or
Clang; MinGW-w64 on Windows) and a 64-bit target. By default make builds
ISA-L 2.32.1 (gzip), zstd 1.5.7 and lz4 1.10.0 from pinned, SHA-256-checked
release tarballs and links them statically, so the binary has no library
dependencies (curl or wget on the first build; NASM 2.16.03 for ISA-L's x86
code if no NASM >= 2.14 is installed).

configure checks whether the compiler can build the AVX2/BMI2 kernels; the
binary uses them when both the CPU and the operating system support them
(CPUID and XGETBV), chosen at run time.

    ./bootstrap             # from a Git checkout (autoconf, automake)
    ./configure
    make                    # ./mkc
    make check              # tests
    make check-full         # check the table layout
    make python             # Python extension + bundled CLI, tested
    make examples           # examples/query, examples/batch
    pip install .           # or install the Python package

| Configure option | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| --disable-simd | build the portable scanner and lookups only |
| --enable-sanitizers | build with ASan and UBSan |
| --with-bundled-deps=no/auto | link the system libraries (auto: if all are present) |
| --host=x86_64-w64-mingw32 | Windows (MinGW-w64), with bundled libraries |

    ./mkc build -k 31 -t 16 -o genome.mkc genome.fa.gz
    ./mkc build -k 21 -o reads.mkc reads_1.fastq.zst reads_2.fastq.zst
    ./mkc info genome.mkc
    ./mkc query genome.mkc ACGTACGTACGTACGTACGTACGTACGTACG
    ./mkc query genome.mkc - < kmers.txt
    ./mkc dump genome.mkc

Options: -v/--version prints the version and the SIMD kernels; -k (default 31),
-m minimizer length (default min(12,max(9,k-8)), at most k), -t
threads (default: online CPUs), -M workspace MiB (default 6400), -T
temporary directory (default TMPDIR, then /tmp; on Windows the user's
temporary directory), --no-sync (skip the final fsync), -o output. Options
precede inputs; use -- before a path beginning with '-'. One '-' input reads
stdin. Multiple inputs accumulate into one index. The output is published
atomically (rename) only after a complete build.

C API (src/mkc.h)
-------------------
Header-only C99, for POSIX or Win32 (MinGW-w64). An open index is immutable
and safe to share between threads. mkc_open reads an index of 64 MiB or more
into memory backed by large pages when it takes at most half of the available
memory and large pages are available (random lookups then avoid most page
walks): transparent huge pages on Linux, MEM_LARGE_PAGES on Windows (needs
the "Lock pages in memory" right), 2 MiB superpages on Intel Macs. Otherwise,
and always with mkc_open_mapped, it maps the file read-only.

    #include "mkc.h"
    mkc_index db;
    if (mkc_open(&db, "genome.mkc")) { /* errno */ }
    uint64_t count;
    if (mkc_count(&db, dna, len, &count)) { /* not k bases of ACGT */ }
    count = mkc_count_key(&db, key);           /* 2-bit packed, A=0..T=3 */
    mkc_count_batch(&db, keys, counts, n);     /* prefetches ahead */
    mkc_count_sequence(&db, read, len, counts);   /* every window of a sequence, batched */
    mkc_iterator it = {0}; uint64_t key;
    while (mkc_next(&db, &it, &key, &count)) { ... }
    /* in parallel: positions 0..mkc_iter_end(&db), split into ranges [a, b) */
    mkc_iterator r = {a};
    while (r.at < b && mkc_next(&db, &r, &key, &count) && r.at-1 < b) { ... }
    mkc_close(&db);

Keys are packed with the first base most significant; either orientation is
accepted. See examples/query.c and examples/batch.c. On x86-64 with AVX2 and
BMI1/BMI2 (and an OS that saves AVX state), opening an index selects lookup
code compiled for its k and record width (224 point kernels, plus 21 for packed
buckets, one per low-part width, and block kernels for the batch API); other CPUs
use the portable path.

Python
------
    import mkc
    idx = mkc.build(["genome.fa.gz"], "genome.mkc", k=31, threads=16)
    idx["ACGTACGTACGTACGTACGTACGTACGTACG"]   # count, either orientation
    idx.count_keys(keys)        # any uint64 buffer (numpy, array('Q')); GIL released
    idx.count_sequence(read)    # count of every k-mer window (0 if it has an N)
    for kmer, count in idx: ...
    len(idx), idx.total, idx.k, idx.layout
    mkc.encode("ACGT"), mkc.decode(27, 4)

mkc.Index(path) opens an existing index (mapped=True to always memory-map
it, see the C API). mkc.build runs the bundled mkc binary ($MKC_BINARY
overrides it).
